WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.230
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:01.870
I am Dr.

3
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:08.350
Cesar Marolla, Instructor at Harvard University Division of Continuing Education.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.350
R M T I turns what we can measure into one score, and follows it over time.

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.950
Here: breast cancer.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.351
Case study: O'Shaughnessy and colleagues, Breast Cancer Research and Treatment, 2026.

7
00:00:20.401 --> 00:00:31.950
In five thousand forty-one women with metastatic hormone-receptor-positive breast cancer, survival after first post-endocrine chemotherapy was thirteen point five months, and thirty percent died before a second-line regimen.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.950
Stage one. A small invasive tumor, with lymph nodes clear or minimally involved.

9
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.950
Stage two. A larger tumor, or involvement of axillary lymph nodes.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.950
Stage three. Extensive nodal disease, or spread to the chest wall or skin.

11
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.950
Stage four. Distant metastasis, for example to bone or liver.

12
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.969
Data assessing.

13
00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:04.503
Prior CDK four six inhibitor use informs likelihood.

14
00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:08.717
Performance status and metastatic burden inform vulnerability.

15
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:13.067
The thirty percent who die before second line inform resilience.

16
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:13.950
Illustrative.

17
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.037
R equals L times E times V times one minus rho.

18
00:01:18.087 --> 00:01:24.559
Here R is zero point zero five four, band two of the fixed five-band scale.

19
00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:27.950
Expected Annual Loss adds consequences.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:28.782
Trajectory.

21
00:01:28.832 --> 00:01:32.942
Each review cycle updates vulnerability and resilience.

22
00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:43.379
This synthetic example closes the delivery gap: R falls from zero point zero five four to zero point zero two three, band two to band one.

23
00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:45.950
An illustration, not a prediction.

24
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.605
What R M T I does.

25
00:01:47.655 --> 00:01:58.180
It quantifies risk as one score, assesses it on fixed bands, and tracks vulnerability and resilience across cycles.

26
00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.950
Treatment decisions stay with clinicians.

27
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.236
R M T I is a research prototype: uncalibrated, synthetic examples, not a medical device.

28
00:02:08.286 --> 00:02:12.307
Does trajectory add information beyond the current level?

29
00:02:12.357 --> 00:02:13.950
Thank you for watching.
